WHAT .text() DOES:
JSoup's .text() method:

✅ Strips Confluence's HTML structure (<div>, <p>, <body>)
❌ DOES NOT strip user content - it extracts it as-is

So if user pastes <script>alert('xss')</script> in Confluence:

Confluence stores it as content
.text() extracts: <script>alert('xss')</script> (because that's the TEXT content)
Gets stored in chunks with script tags intact


IS THIS A BUG?
NO - THIS IS CORRECT BEHAVIOR because:

✅ User intentionally added that content (they pasted it)
✅ Code examples need preservation (JavaScript books)
✅ Content should match source (faithful to Confluence)


IS THIS A SECURITY RISK?
ONLY IF:

❌ You expose a raw chunk endpoint without escaping
❌ You render chunks directly in HTML without sanitization
❌ Local LLM returns raw content instead of descriptions

SOLUTION:

✅ Never render raw chunk content in HTML
✅ Always escape if displaying chunks
✅ Secure any /api/chunks/{id} endpoint with HTML escaping


